artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a continuous-time linear periodic system of the form
   dx(t)/dt = A(t) x(t) + B(t) u(t),   y(t) = C(t) x(t) + D(t) u(t),
where A(t), B(t), C(t), D(t) are real matrix-valued functions of time, periodic with common period T>0, with x(t) ∈ R^n, u(t) ∈ R^m, y(t) ∈ R^p. Expand each periodic coefficient matrix in a Fourier series, e.g. A(t) = Σ_{k ∈ Z} A_k e^{i k ω t} with ω = 2π/T, and likewise for B, C, D. The frequency-lifted (Toeplitz) representation of the system replaces (A, B, C, D) by the doubly infinite block matrices
   𝓐 = blockToeplitz((A_{j-k}))_{j,k ∈ Z} − i ω · diag(k I_n)_{k ∈ Z},
   𝓑 = blockToeplitz((B_{j-k})),  𝓒 = blockToeplitz((C_{j-k})),  𝓓 = blockToeplitz((D_{j-k})),
which, after truncation to indices |k| ≤ N, yields large structured matrices having block Toeplitz or block Toeplitz-plus-block-diagonal structure.

Problem. Develop numerically reliable, structure-exploiting and/or structure-preserving algorithms operating directly on this frequency-lifted block-Toeplitz (or block-Toeplitz-plus-diagonal) representation for the standard computational tasks associated with periodic linear systems — including:
 (i) computing characteristic exponents (Floquet/Lyapunov spectrum) and determining stability;
 (ii) solving periodic Lyapunov and Riccati differential equations;
 (iii) computing periodic system norms (H_2, H_∞);
 (iv) realization, balancing, and model reduction;
 (v) periodic state-feedback and observer design (pole placement, LQ/LQG, H_∞ control).

The required algorithms must (a) exploit the block-Toeplitz / Toeplitz-plus-diagonal structure to achieve substantially better-than-cubic complexity in the truncation parameter N, (b) preserve this structure under the relevant matrix operations and factorizations whenever theoretically appropriate, and (c) come with rigorous accuracy and convergence analysis as N → ∞.

